ugh many points? For example, line y = x (slope 1) passes through (1,1),(2,2),(3,3) but (3,3) is not in the set because 3+3=6 >5? Actually (3,3) sum=6, which is not ≤5, so not in the set. So line y=x passes through (1,1),(2,2). That's 2 points. Not many. Line y = 2x - 1 passes through (1,1),(2,3). That's 2 points. Many lines can pass through at most 2 points because the points are not collinear except for vertical/horizontal/diagonal lines. So we cannot cover many points with a sunny line. So covering 10 points with 2 sunny lines is impossible. So k=2 is impossible for n=5.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k=2 is impossible seems plausible. But we need to verify the reasoning. The solution's reasoning: \"Column x=n-2 has 3 points, forcing a vertical line. Then analyzing columns x=n-1 and x=n shows that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). This forces a third sunny line, contradicting k=2.\" Let's parse that.\n\nThe solution says: Column x=n-2 has 3 points, forcing a vertical line. Indeed, we argued that we need a vertical line for column n-2. So we have at least one vertical line (non-sunny). Then they consider columns x=n-1 and x=n. Column x=n-1 has points (n-1,1),(n-1,2). Column x=n has point (n,1). They claim that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). Let's examine: If we try to cover (n,1) and (n-1,1) with a line, that's horizontal y=1, slope 0, non-sunny. If we try to cover (n,1) and (n-1,2) with a line, slope = (1-2)/(n-(n-1)) = -1/1 = -1, slope -1, non-sunny. So indeed any line that covers (n,1) and any point in column n-1 is either horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny. So a sunny line cannot cover (n,1) together with any point in column n-1. So to cover (n,1) we need a line that only covers (n,1) (maybe also other points not in column n-1). But we have o