ot cover any two of them with a sunny line. So we need at least three lines to cover them, but we only have two sunny lines left. So impossible. So for n=3, k=2 is impossible. So the solution's claim holds for n=3.\n\nNow for n≥4, we need to see if k=2 is possible. The solution's argument uses column n-2 forcing a vertical line, then analyzing columns n-1 and n shows that point (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1 (any such line is horizontal or slope -1, both non-sunny). This forces a third sunny line, contradicting k=2. Let's examine that reasoning more carefully.\n\nWe have a vertical line covering column n-2. So we have used one non-sunny line. We have n-2 non-sunny lines total, so we have n-3 non-sunny lines left. We have 2 sunny lines left. We need to cover columns n-1 and n. Column n-1 has points (n-1,1) and (n-1,2). Column n has point (n,1). The vertical line covering column n-2 does not cover any of these points. So we need to cover these three points with the remaining lines. The solution says that (n,1) cannot be covered by a sunny line together with points in column n-1, because any line connecting (n,1) to (n-1,1) is horizontal (non-sunny), and any line connecting (n,1) to (n-1,2) is slope -1 (non-sunny). So a sunny line cannot cover (n,1) together with any point in column n-1. So to cover (n,1) we need a line that does not contain any point from column n-1. That line could be a non-sunny line (vertical x=n) or a sunny line that covers (n,1) and some other points not in column n-1. But we have only 2 sunny lines total. If we use a sunny line to cover (n,1) alone (or with other points not in column n-1), then we still need to cover the two points in column n-1. Could we cover both points in column n-1 with a single line? The line through (n-1,1) and (n-1,2) is vertical, non-sunny. So we cannot use a sunny line to cover both. So we need at least two lines to cover the two points in column n-1. Those lines could be non