n 5 must pass through (5,1). But we have 4 lines, and each line can intersect column 5 at most once. So at most 4 lines can intersect column 5, but there is only one point (5,1). So only one line can cover (5,1). The other lines cannot intersect column 5 (or they would intersect at a point not in the set). So we need to assign (5,1) to exactly one line, say L1. The other lines will not have points in column 5.\n\nNow column 4 has two points: (4,1) and (4,2). We have 4 lines, each can intersect column 4 at most once. So we need to assign these two points to two distinct lines, say L2 and L3. The remaining line L4 will not intersect column 4.\n\nColumn 3 has three points: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We have 4 lines, each can intersect column 3 at most once. So we need to assign these three points to three distinct lines, say L1, L2, L3 (or any). The remaining line L4 will not intersect column 3.\n\nColumn 2 has four points: (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4). We have 4 lines, each must intersect column 2 exactly once (since we need to cover all points). So each line L_i must pass through a distinct point in column 2. So we have a bijection between lines and points in column 2.\n\nThus we need to assign each line L_i to a point in column 2 (its base point), and also assign it to at most one point in each of columns 3,4,5, such that the points assigned to L_i are collinear. So we need to find a set of 4 lines L_i such that:\n\n- L_i passes through (2,i) (i=1,2,3,4).\n- L_i also passes through a point in column 3 (maybe none for some lines) and a point in column 4 (maybe none) and a point in column 5 (maybe none), but the assignments must be consistent: each point in column 3,4,5 must be assigned to exactly one line.\n\nWe need to find slopes m_i such that for each line L_i, the points (j, y) = (j, i + m_i (j-2)) are integer and within allowed range for the columns we assign.\n\nLet's try to find such slopes.\n\nWe have 4 lines, we need to assign:\n\n- (5,1) to some line L_a.\n- (4,1) and (4,2) to two lines L_b and L_c.\n- (3,1),(3,2),(3,3) to three lines L_d, L_e, L_f (some may overlap with previous assignments).\n- (2,i) for i=1..4 are base points.\n\nWe need to assign each line to at most one point per column. So each line can have at most one of the points in column 3, at most one in column 4, at most one in column 5.\n\nThus we need to assign the points in columns 3,4,5 to distinct lines, but we have 4 lines, and there are 1+2+3 = 6 points to assign. Since each line can take at most one point per column, a line can take up to 3 points (one from each column). So we could assign up to 12 points across 4 lines, but we only have 6 points, so it's possible.\n\nWe need to find slopes m_i such that for each line L_i, the points assigned are collinear. Let's try to assign:\n\n- L1: base (2,1). Let's assign it to (3,2) (col 3) and (4,3) (col 4) and (5,4) (col 5). But (4,3) and (5,4) are not allowed (out of range). So not possible.\n\n- L1: base (2,1). Could assign it to (3,2) and (4,2) and (5,1). Let's see if there is a line through (2,1), (3,2), (4,2), (5,1). Check if these points are collinear. Compute slopes: slope between (2,1) and (3,2) = (2-1)/(3-2) = 1. Slope between (3,2) and (4,2) = (2-2)/(4-3) = 0, not equal to 1, so not collinear. So cannot have both (3,2) and (4,2) on same line with (2,1). So we need to assign at most one point per column to each line.\n\nThus each line can have at most one point from each column, but they must be collinear. So we need to find a line through (2,i) that passes through exactly one point in each of columns 3,4,5 (maybe none for some columns). So we need to find slopes m_i such that the points (j, i + m_i (j-2)) are integer and within allowed range for each j we want to assign.\n\nLet's try to find integer slopes m_i for each i.\n\nWe have base points (2,i). For each i, we can choose a slope m_i (rational) such that for j=3,4,5, the y-values are integer and within allowed range.\n\nWe need to assign each point in column 3 to a distinct line. So we need to choose for each i a slope m_i such that i + m_i (3-2) = i + m_i is an integer in {1,2,3} and distinct across i for the three lines that we assign to column 3. Similarly for column 4: i + m_i (4-2) = i + 2 m_i must be integer in {1,2} and distinct across lines assigned to column 4. For column 5: i + 3 m_i must be integer = 1 (since only point (5,1)). So for the line that covers (5,1), we need i + 3 m_i = 1 => m_i = (1 - i)/3. Since i is integer 1..4, m_i will be rational. For i=1, m_i = 0; slope 0 is horizontal, not allowed (sunny lines cannot be horizontal). So i=1 cannot be assigned to (5,1). For i=2, m_i = (1-2)/3 = -1/3, slope -1/3 is allowed (not 0, -1, infinite). For i=3, m_i = (1-3)/3 = -2/3, allowed. For i=4, m_i = (1-4)/3 = -1, slope -1 is not allowed. So i=4 cannot be assigned to (5,1). So the line covering (5,1) must be either L2 (i=2) with slope -1/3, or L3 (i=3) with slope -2/3. Let's pick L2 (i=2) with slope -1/3. Then L2 passes through (2,2) and (5,1). Check intermediate columns: at x=3, y = 2 + (-1/3)*(3-2) = 2 - 1/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666..., not integer, so L2 does not pass through any integer point in column 3. At x=4, y = 2 + (-1/3)*(4-2) = 2 - 2/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333..., not integer. So L2 only covers (2,2) and (5,1). That's okay; we don't need it to cover points in columns 3 and 4.\n\nNow we need to cover column 4 points (4,1) and (4,2). We have lines L1 (i=1), L3 (i=3), L4 (i=4) left. We need to assign each of these points to a distinct line. Let's try to assign (4,1) to L1 (i=1). For L1, we need a slope m1 such that at x=4, y = 1 + m1*(4-2) = 1 + 2 m1 = 1. So 2 m1 = 0 => m1 = 0. That's horizontal, not allowed. So L1 cannot cover (4,1). Try L1 covering (4,2): 1 + 2 m1 = 2 => 2 m1 = 1 => m1 = 0.5. That's slope 1/2, allowed. So L1 can have slope 1/2 and pass through (2,1) and (4,2). Check column 3: at x=3, y = 1 + (1/2)*(3-2) = 1 + 0.5 = 1.5, not integer, so L1 does not cover any point in column 3. That's okay. So L1 can be assigned to (4,2). So L1: base (2,1), slope 1/2, passes through (4,2). Good.\n\nNow we need to cover (4,1) with either L3 (i=3) or L4 (i=4). Let's try L3 (i=3). Need slope m3 such that at x=4, y = 3 + m3*(4-2) = 3 + 2 m3 = 1 => 2 m3 = -2 => m3 = -1. That's slope -1, not allowed. So L3 cannot cover (4,1). Try L4 (i=4). Need slope m4 such that 4 + 2 m4 = 1 => 2 m4 = -3 => m4 = -1.5 = -3/2, allowed (not -1). So L4 can have slope -3/2 and pass through (2,4) and (4,1). Check column 3: at x=3, y = 4 + (-3/2)*(3-2) = 4 - 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. So L4 does not cover any point in column 3. That's okay.\n\nNow we have covered column 4 points: (4,2) by L1, (4,1) by L4.\n\nNow we need to cover column 3 points: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3). We have lines L1, L2, L3, L4. L1 currently covers (2,1) and (4,2). It does not intersect column 3 at integer point. L2 covers (2,2) and (5,1). It does not intersect column 3 at integer point. L4 covers (2,4) and (4,1). It does not intersect column 3 at integer point. So we have L3 left (i=3) not assigned yet. L3 can be used to cover some column 3 points. But we have three points to cover, and only one line left (L3). However, a line can intersect column 3 at only one point. So we cannot cover all three points with a single line. So we need to assign some of the other lines to also intersect column 3 at integer points. But we previously said L1, L2, L4 do not intersect column 3 at integer points. However, we could adjust slopes for L1, L2, L4 to also intersect column 3 at integer points, while still covering the assigned points in columns 4 and 5. Let's see if we can adjust.\n\nWe need to assign each line to at most one point per column, but we can assign each line to a point in column 3 as well, as long as the line passes through that point. So we need to find slopes for L1, L2, L4 that satisfy both the column 4 assignment and also pass through a column 3 point (maybe different from the column 4 point). However, a line cannot pass through two points in the same column (since that would be vertical). So each line can intersect column 3 at at most one point. So we need to assign each of the three column 3 points to three distinct lines among L1, L2, L3, L4. L3 is free, so we can assign one of them to L3. The other two must be assigned to two of L1, L2, L4. So we need to adjust slopes for those lines to pass through a column 3 point while still passing through their assigned column 4 or column 5 point.\n\nLet's see if we can adjust L1: L1 currently passes through (2,1) and (4,2). That determines the line uniquely: slope = (2-1)/(4-2) = 1/2. So L1 is fixed. It passes through (3, y) where y = 1 + (1/2)*(3-2) = 1.5, not integer. So L1 cannot be adjusted to also pass through an integer point in column 3 while still passing through (4,2). So L1 cannot cover any column 3 point. So we cannot assign a column 3 point to L1.\n\nNow L4: passes through (2,4) and (4,1). Slope = (1-4)/(4-2) = -3/2. So L4 is fixed. At x=3, y = 4 + (-3/2)*(3-2) = 4 - 1.5 = 2.5, not integer. So L4 cannot cover any column 3 point.\n\nL2: passes through (2,2) and (5,1). Slope = (1-2)/(5-2) = -1/3. So L2 is fixed. At x=3, y = 2 + (-1/3)*(3-2) = 2 - 1/3 = 5/3 ≈ 1.666..., not integer. At x=4, y = 2 + (-1/3)*(4-2) = 2 - 2/3 = 4/3 ≈ 1.333..., not integer. So L2 cannot cover any column 3 point.\n\nThus L1, L2, L4 cannot cover any column 3 point. So we only have L3 left to cover column 3 points, which is insufficient. So this assignment fails.\n\nThus we need a different assignment. Perhaps we need to assign column 4 points to lines that also intersect column 3 at integer points. Let's try a different approach: assign column 4 points to lines that also intersect column 3 at integer points. For each line, we need to satisfy two constraints: pass through a point in column 4 and a point in column 3 (maybe also column 5). That determines the line uniquely (if we pick two points). So we need to find pairs (col3 point, col4 point) such that the line connecting them also passes through a point in column 2 (the base point). Since we have base points (2,i) for i=1..4, we need to match each line to a base point. So we need to find a matching between base points and pairs (col3, col4) such that the line through the base point and the col3 point also passes through the col4 point (or vice versa). Actually we need a line that passes through three points: base point (2,i), a point in column 3 (3,b3), and a point in column 4 (4,b4). That would be a line determined by any two of them, but we need all three to be collinear. So we need to find triples (i,b3,b4) such that points (2,i), (3,b3), (4,b4) are collinear and the slopes are not 0, -1, infinite. Let's try to find such triples.\n\nGeneral condition: Points (2,i), (3,b3), (4,b4) are collinear if the slope between (2,i) and (3,b3) equals the slope between (3,b3) and (4,b4). Slope between (2,i) and (3,b3) = (b3 - i) / (3-2) = b3 - i. Slope between (3,b3) and (4,b4) = (b4 - b3) / (4-3) = b4 - b3. So collinearity condition: b3 - i = b4 - b3 => b4 = 2 b3 - i.\n\nThus for each base point i, we need to choose b3 (in {1,2,3}) and then b4 = 2 b3 - i must be in {1,2} (since column 4 allowed y values are 1 or 2). Also b3 must be integer and within allowed range for column 3 (1 ≤ b3 ≤ 3). Also b4 must be integer and within allowed range for column 4 (1 ≤ b4 ≤ 2). Also the slope must not be 0, -1, infinite. The slope is b3 - i. So we need b3 - i ≠ 0, -1. Also slope cannot be infinite (vertical) because we have distinct x coordinates.\n\nThus we need to find for each i ∈ {1,2,3,4} a b3 ∈ {1,2,3} such that b4 = 2 b3 - i ∈ {1,2} and b3 - i ≠ 0, -1.\n\nLet's try to find such triples.\n\nFor i=1: b3 - 1 ≠ 0 => b3 ≠ 1. b3 - 1 ≠ -1 => b3 ≠ 0 (automatically). So b3 can be 2 or 3. Let's try b3=2: then b4 = 2*2 - 1 = 3. But b4=3 is not allowed (col4 max y=2). So b3=2 fails. b3=3: b4 = 2*3 - 1 = 5, not allowed. So i=1 cannot have a triple covering both col3 and col4.\n\nThus line through base (2,1) cannot pass through a point in column 3 and a point in column 4 simultaneously while staying within allowed ranges. So L1 cannot cover both a column 3 and column 4 point. It can cover at most one of them.\n\nFor i=2: b3 - 2 ≠ 0 => b3 ≠ 2. b3 - 2 ≠ -1 => b3 ≠ 1. So b3 cannot be 1 or 2, so b3 must be 3. Then b4 = 2*3 - 2 = 4, not allowed (col4 max y=2). So i=2 cannot have a triple covering both col3 and col4.\n\nFor i=3: b3 - 3 ≠ 0 => b3 ≠ 3. b3 - 3 ≠ -1 => b3 ≠ 2. So b3 must be 1. Then b4 = 2*1 - 3 = -1, not allowed (must be ≥1). So i=3 cannot have a triple covering both col3 and col4.\n\nFor i=4: b3 - 4 ≠ 0 => b3 ≠ 4 (not possible anyway). b3 - 4 ≠ -1 => b3 ≠ 3. So b3 can be 1 or 2. Let's try b3=1: then b4 = 2*1 - 4 = -2, not allowed. b3=2: b4 = 2*2 - 4 = 0, not allowed (must be ≥1). So i=4 cannot have a triple covering both col3 and col4.\n\nThus no line through a base point (2,i) can pass through a point in column 3 and a point in column 4 simultaneously while staying within allowed ranges. So we cannot have a line that covers both a column 3 point and a column 4 point. So each line can cover at most one of the columns 3 or 4 (or maybe both if we allow column 5 as well). But we have 4 lines and need to cover 3 points in column 3 and 2 points in column 4. So we need at least 3 lines for column 3 and at least 2 lines for column 4. Since each line can cover at most one point in each column, we need at least max(3,2) = 3 lines, but we have 4 lines, so that's okay. However, we need to assign the points such that each line covers at most one point per column. So we can assign 3 lines to cover the 3 column 3 points, and 2 lines to cover the 2 column 4 points. Since we have 4 lines, we can assign some lines to cover both a column 3 point and a column 4 point? But we just saw that no line can cover both a column 3 and column 4 point simultaneously. So each line can cover at most one of those columns. So we need at least 3+2 = 5 lines to cover all points in columns 3 and 4, if each line can cover at most one point in each column and cannot cover both columns. But we have only 4 lines. So it's impossible. Wait, we need to be careful: each line can cover at most one point per column, but it can cover a point in column 3 and also a point in column 4 (different columns). The earlier analysis shows that a line through a base point cannot pass through a point in column 3 and a point in column 4 simultaneously while staying within allowed ranges. But maybe a line that does not pass through a base point (i.e., not through column 2) could cover both a column 3 and column 4 point. However, we have only 4 lines total, and we need to cover column 2 points as well. Since we have 4 points in column 2, each line must pass through a distinct point in column 2 (since we have only 4 lines). So each line must pass through a point in column 2. So each line is determined by its base point in column 2. So any line must pass through a point (2,i). So the earlier analysis applies: any line must pass through a base point (2,i). So we cannot have a line that covers both a column 3 and column 4 point. So each line can cover at most one point among columns 3 and 4. So we need at least 3+2 = 5 lines to cover all points in columns 3 and 4. Since we have only 4 lines, it's impossible. Therefore k=4 is impossible for n=5.\n\nThus the solution's claim that k≥4 is impossible seems plausible. However, we need to verify for general n. The solution's reasoning \"sunny lines cover at most one point per column\" is insufficient to prove impossibility for k≥4, but we can argue more strongly: For k≥4, we have n-k non-sunny lines, which can be vertical lines covering columns 1,...,n-k. Then we need to cover columns n-k+1,...,n with k sunny lines. The number of points in column n-k+1 is k, column n-k+2 has k-1, ..., column n has 1. So total points in these columns is k + (k-1) + ... + 1 = k(k+1)/2. We have k lines to cover these points. Each line can intersect each column at most once, so each line can cover at most one point per column. So the total number of points that can be covered by k lines across these columns is at most k * (number of columns) = k * k = k^2 (since there are k columns). Since k(k+1)/2 ≤ k^2 for k≥1, capacity is enough. So capacity is not a problem. However, we also need to consider that each line must intersect column n-k+1 at a distinct point (since there are k points in that column). So each line must be assigned a distinct point in column n-k+1. So each line is determined by its intersection with column n-k+1. Then we need to see if each line can also intersect column n-k+2 at a point (maybe distinct). For each line, we need to assign a point in column n-k+2, but there are k-1 points in that column, so we need to assign them to k-1 of the k lines, leaving one line not intersecting column n-k+2. That's okay. Similarly for column n-k+3, we have k-2 points, assign them to k-2 lines, etc. So we need to assign points in each column to distinct lines, but each line can intersect each column at most once. So we need to find a set of k lines such that for each column j (n-k+1 ≤ j ≤ n), the points in that column are assigned to distinct lines, and each line passes through the assigned points across columns. This is essentially constructing a set of k lines that pass through a \"triangular\" arrangement of points: one point in each column for each line, forming a \"Latin triangle\". This is reminiscent of constructing a set of k lines that intersect each column at distinct points, forming a \"grid\" of points. This is possible if we can find a set of k lines that intersect each column at distinct points. This is essentially constructing a set of k lines that are not parallel to each other (since they intersect each column at distinct points). Actually they could be parallel, but then they'd intersect each column at the same y coordinate, which would cause collisions. So they must be distinct lines.\n\nIs it always possible to find k lines that intersect each column at distinct points? For k=3, we found a construction. For k=4, we attempted for n=5 and found impossible due to constraints. But maybe for larger n, k=4 might be possible. Let's test n=6, k=4. Then n-k = 2 non-sunny lines, which can be vertical lines covering columns 1 and 2. Then we need to cover columns 3,4,5,6 with 4 sunny lines. Column 3 has points (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4) (since n+1 - 3 = 4). Column 4 has (4,1),(4,2),(4,3). Column 5 has (5,1),(5,2). Column 6 has (6,1). So we have 4 columns, with point counts: 4,3,2,1. Total points = 10. We have 4 lines. Each line can intersect each column at most once, so each line can cover at most 4 points (one per column). So total capacity = 4*4 = 16 > 10, so capacity is fine. We need to assign points to lines such that each line passes through a point in each column (maybe not all columns). The assignment must be such that each line is determined by its intersection with column 3 (since there are 4 points in column 3, we need to assign each of the 4 lines to a distinct point in column 3). So each line passes through a distinct point (3,b3_i) where b3_i ∈ {1,2,3,4}. Then we need to assign points in column 4 to lines: there are 3 points (4,1),(4,2),(4,3). So we need to assign each of these to a distinct line among the 4 lines, leaving one line not intersecting column 4. Similarly for column 5 (2 points) assign to 2 lines, column 6 (1 point) assign to 1 line. So we need to find lines that pass through the assigned points. This is similar to constructing a set of 4 lines that pass through a \"triangular\" arrangement of points: we need to find a set of 4 lines such that the points (3,b3_i) are on them, and also each line passes through a point in column 4 (maybe not all lines), etc. This is reminiscent of constructing a \"complete set of lines\" that intersect each column at distinct points. This is essentially constructing a set of lines that form a \"grid\" of points: we need to find a set of k lines that intersect each of the k columns at distinct points, forming a \"Latin rectangle\" of size k x k. This is possible if we can find a set of k lines with distinct slopes (or at least distinct intersection patterns) such that the intersection points with each column are distinct. This is reminiscent of the problem of constructing a set of k lines that intersect a set of k vertical lines (the columns) at distinct points, which is always possible: choose lines with slopes m_i and intercepts b_i such that the intersection with x = j is y = m_i * (j - x0) + y0? Actually we can param: line L_i: y = a_i x + c_i. Intersection with column x=j is y = a_i j + c_i. For each column j, we need the values a_i j + c_i to be distinct for i=1..k (to cover all points in that column). Also we need these values to be within the allowed range for that column (i.e., between 1 and n+1 - j). So we need to find a_i, c_i such that for each j, the set {a_i j + c_i} (i=1..k) is a permutation of the allowed y-values for that column (which are 1,...,n+1 - j). This is a system of equations. For k=4, we need to find 4 lines such that for j=3, the y-values are {1,2,3,4} (some permutation). For j=4, the y-values are {1,2,3} (some subset of 3 of the 4 lines). For j=5, y-values are {1,2} (some subset of 2 lines). For j=6, y-value is {1} (some line). This is reminiscent of constructing a Latin square of order k, but with decreasing sets. It might be possible for k=4 and n=6. Let's try to find explicit lines.\n\nWe need lines L_i: y = a_i x + b_i (with a_i not equal to 0, -1, infinite). Since we cannot have vertical lines, we need finite slopes. Also slopes cannot be 0 (horizontal) or -1. So a_i ∈ ℝ \\ {0, -1}. Also we need distinct lines (distinct a_i or b_i). Let's try to find integer slopes and intercepts that work.\n\nWe need for x=3: y-values must be {1,2,3,4}. So we need four lines such that a_i * 3 + b_i = y_i ∈ {1,2,3,4} distinct. For x=4: a_i * 4 + b_i must be in {1,2,3} for three of the lines, and the fourth line must produce a y-value outside the allowed range (i.e., ≤0 or > n+1 - 4 = 3). For x=5: a_i * 5 + b_i must be in {1,2} for two lines, and the other two lines produce out-of-range values. For x=6: a_i * 6 + b_i must be 1 for one line, and the other three produce out-of-range values.\n\nWe can try to find integer slopes a_i and integer intercepts b_i that satisfy these constraints. Let's try to set up a system.\n\nLet’s denote L_i: y = a_i x + c_i. For each i, we need to assign a column j_i where the line is \"active\" (i.e., its intersection is within the allowed range). For each line, we can decide which columns it will intersect within the allowed range. For simplicity, we can try to assign each line to intersect all columns (i.e., produce a valid y for each column). But that may be impossible because the allowed y ranges shrink as x increases. However, we could assign each line to intersect only a subset of columns.\n\nAlternatively, we can try to find a construction using a known combinatorial design: For k lines, we can use lines of the form y = i x + (some constant) such that the intersection points form a \"grid\" of points (i, j) mapping to (x=j, y = i j + c_i). But we need the y-values to be within the allowed range. This is reminiscent of constructing a Latin rectangle of size k x k with entries from 1..k, but with decreasing column sizes. This might be possible for any k, but we need to check if slopes can be chosen to avoid 0, -1.\n\nLet's try to find a general construction for any k: Use lines L_i: y = i x - i(i-1)/2. Let's test for n = 2k-1? Actually we need n≥3, but we can choose n = 2k-1 maybe. Let's see.\n\nWe need to cover columns from n-k+1 to n. For n = 2k-1, n-k+1 = k. So columns are k, k+1, ..., 2k-1. The number of columns is k. The column j (where j runs from k to 2k-1) has points (j,b) where b ≤ n+1 - j = (2k-1)+1 - j = 2k - j. So column j has 2k - j points. For j = k, column k has 2k - k = k points. For j = k+1, column k+1 has k-1 points, etc., down to column 2k-1 has 1 point. So the pattern matches the triangular shape we need.\n\nNow consider lines L_i: y = i x - i(i-1)/2. For x=j, y = i j - i(i-1)/2. For j = k, y = i k - i(i-1)/2. We want these y-values to be exactly the numbers 1,...,k (in some order). Let's see if we can choose i from 1 to k such that y_i = i k - i(i-1)/2 yields a permutation of {1,...,k}. Compute y_i = i k - i(i-1)/2 = i(k - (i-1)/2). For i=1: y_1 = 1*k - 0 = k. For i=2: y_2 = 2k - 2*1/2 = 2k - 1 = 2k-1. That's > k for k≥2. So not in {1,...,k}. So that doesn't work.\n\nAlternatively, we can try lines L_i: y = (i-1) x + (k+1-i). For i=1,...,k. Then at x = k, y = (i-1)k + (k+1-i) = (i-1)k + k+1 - i = (i-1)k + k + 1 - i = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + k - i + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1)k + (k - i) + 1 = (i-1