 (since we need total 4 sunny lines). Can we cover these 4 points with a single sunny line? Let's see if any line passes through at least 2 of them. (2,3) and (2,4) vertical, not allowed. (2,3) and (4,1) slope = (1-3)/(4-2) = -2/2 = -1, not allowed. (2,3) and (5,1) slope = (1-3)/(5-2) = -2/3, which is sunny. So line through (2,3) and (5,1) slope -2/3. Does it also pass through (2,4) or (4,1)? Let's check (4,1): y = -2/3*(4-2)+3 = -2/3*2+3 = -4/3+3 = 5/3 ≈1.666, not 1. So (4,1) not on that line. (2,4) is not on that line because x=2 gives y=3 (the line passes through (2,3)). So we cannot cover all 4 points with one line. Could we choose a different set of lines? Perhaps we can use a line with slope -2 covering (2,4),(3,2),(4,0?) but (4,0) not allowed. Let's systematically search for a covering with 4 sunny lines.\n\nWe need to cover 10 points with 4 lines, each line can cover at most maybe 3 points. So we need at least 4 lines covering 10 points, average 2.5 points per line. So we need at least some lines covering 3 points. Let's find lines that cover 3 points.\n\nPotential lines with slope -2: y = -2x + c. For points in columns x=2..5, compute c = y + 2x:\n\n(2,1): 1+4=5\n(2,2): 2+4=6\n(2,3): 3+4=7\n(2,4): 4+4=8\n(3,1): 1+6=7\n(3,2): 2+6=8\n(3,3): 3+6=9\n(4,1): 1+8=9\n(4,2): 2+8=10\n(5,1): 1+10=11\n\nSo c values: 5,6,7,8,7,8,9,9,10,11. So lines with same c:\n\nc=7: (2,3) and (3,1) -> 2 points.\nc=8: (2,4) and (3,2) -> 2 points.\nc=9: (3,3) and (4,1) -> 2 points.\nc=10: (4,2) only.\nc=11: (5,1) only.\nc=5: (2,1) only.\nc=6: (2,2) only.\n\nThus slope -2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nWhat about slope 2? y = 2x + c. Compute c = y - 2x:\n\n(2,1): 1-4=-3\n(2,2): 2-4=-2\n(2,3): 3-4=-1\n(2,4): 4-4=0\n(3,1): 1-6=-5\n(3,2): 2-6=-4\n(3,3): 3-6=-3\n(4,1): 1-8=-7\n(4,2): 2-8=-6\n(5,1): 1-10=-9\n\nc values: -3 appears for (2,1) and (3,3). So line y=2x-3 passes through (2,1) and (3,3). That's 2 points. c=-2: (2,2) only. c=-1: (2,3) only. c=0: (2,4) only. c=-5: (3,1) only. c=-4: (3,2) only. c=-7: (4,1) only. c=-6: (4,2) only. c=-9: (5,1) only.\n\nThus slope 2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nWhat about slope 1/2? y = (1/2)x + c. Compute c = y - (1/2)x = (2y - x)/2. For integer points, 2y - x must be even. Let's compute 2y - x for each point:\n\n(2,1): 2-2=0 => c=0\n(2,2): 4-2=2 => c=1\n(2,3): 6-2=4 => c=2\n(2,4): 8-2=6 => c=3\n(3,1): 2-3=-1 => c=-0.5 (not integer but okay)\n(3,2): 4-3=1 => c=0.5\n(3,3): 6-3=3 => c=1.5\n(4,1): 2-4=-2 => c=-1\n(4,2): 4-4=0 => c=0\n(5,1): 2-5=-3 => c=-1.5\n\nSo lines with slope 1/2 can have c values 0,1,2,3,0.5,1.5,-0.5,-1,-1.5. Points sharing same c: c=0: (2,1) and (4,2). So line y = (1/2)x passes through (2,1) and (4,2). That's 2 points. c=1: (2,2) only. c=2: (2,3) only. c=3: (2,4) only. c=0.5: (3,2) only. c=1.5: (3,3) only. c=-0.5: (3,1) only. c=-1: (4,1) only. c=-1.5: (5,1) only.\n\nThus slope 1/2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nWhat about slope -1/2? y = -0.5x + c. Compute c = y + 0.5x = (2y + x)/2. For each point:\n\n(2,1): 2+2=4 => c=2\n(2,2): 4+2=6 => c=3\n(2,3): 6+2=8 => c=4\n(2,4): 8+2=10 => c=5\n(3,1): 2+3=5 => c=2.5\n(3,2): 4+3=7 => c=3.5\n(3,3): 6+3=9 => c=4.5\n(4,1): 2+4=6 => c=3\n(4,2): 4+4=8 => c=4\n(5,1): 2+5=7 => c=3.5\n\nSo c values: 2: (2,1) only; 3: (2,2) and (4,1) -> line y = -0.5x + 3 passes through (2,2) and (4,1). That's 2 points. c=4: (2,3) and (4,2) -> line y = -0.5x + 4 passes through (2,3) and (4,2). That's 2 points. c=5: (2,4) only. c=2.5: (3,1) only. c=3.5: (3,2) and (5,1) -> line y = -0.5x + 3.5 passes through (3,2) and (5,1). That's 2 points. c=4.5: (3,3) only.\n\nThus slope -1/2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nThus many slopes cover at most 2 points. However slope 1 lines can cover up to 3 points for n≥5? Let's check for n=5: slope 1 line y = x + c. For points in columns x=2..5, differences b-a:\n\n(2,1): -1\n(2,2): 0\n(2,3): 1\n(2,4): 2\n(3,1): -2\n(3,2): -1\n(3,3): 0\n(4,1): -3\n(4,2): -2\n(5,1): -4\n\nSo slope 1 lines can cover at most 2 points (differences -1: (2,1),(3,2); diff 0: (2,2),(3,3); diff -2: (3,1),(4,2); diff -3: (4,1) only; diff -4: (5,1) only; diff 1: (2,3) only; diff 2: (2,4) only). So for n=5, slope 1 lines cover at most 2 points. For n=6, slope 1 lines can cover up to 3 points: differences b-a = 0 includes (1,1),(2,2),(3,3). But for columns x≥2, we have (2,2),(3,3),(4,4?) but (4,4) not allowed because 4+4=8 >7? Actually for n=6, a+b ≤7, so (4,4) not allowed. So slope 1 line y=x covers (2,2),(3,3) only (2 points). Wait (1,1) is not in the set after we removed column x=1 (vertical line). So slope 1 line can cover at most 2 points for columns x≥2 for n=5 and n=6? Let's check n=6: columns x=2..6. Points:\n\nx=2: b=1..5 (since 2+b ≤7 => b ≤5) => (2,1),(2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(2,5)\nx=3: b=1..4 => (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4)\nx=4: b=1..3 => (4,1),(4,2),(4,3)\nx=5: b=1..2 => (5,1),(5,2)\nx=6: b=1 => (6,1)\n\nNow slope 1 line y=x + c: differences b-a:\n\n(2,1): -1\n(2,2): 0\n(2,3): 1\n(2,4): 2\n(2,5): 3\n(3,1): -2\n(3,2): -1\n(3,3): 0\n(3,4): 1\n(4,1): -3\n(4,2): -2\n(4,3): -1\n(5,1): -4\n(5,2): -3\n(6,1): -5\n\nSo diff 0: (2,2),(3,3) only (2 points). diff -1: (2,1),(3,2),(4,3) => 3 points! Indeed (4,3) is allowed because 4+3=7 ≤7. So slope 1 line with diff -1 (i.e., y = x - 1) passes through (2,1),(3,2),(4,3). That's 3 points. So slope 1 lines can cover up to 3 points for n≥6. Similarly diff 1: (2,3),(3,4) only 2 points. diff -2: (3,1),(4,2) only 2 points. diff -3: (4,1),(5,2) only 2 points. diff -4: (5,1) only. diff -5: (6,1) only. diff 2: (2,4),(3,5?) but (3,5) not allowed because 3+5=8 >7. So diff 2 only (2,4). diff 3: (2,5) only.\n\nThus for n=6, slope 1 lines can cover up to 3 points. So a sunny line can cover up to 3 points. For n=7, maybe up to 4 points? Let's check pattern: For n, the rightmost column is x=n, with point (n,1). The line y = x - (n-1) passes through points (n-1,? )? Actually y = x - (n-1) yields points (a,b) where b = a - (n-1). For a = n-2, b = (n-2) - (n-1) = -1, not allowed. So that line only passes through (n,1) maybe? Let's not go there. But we can see that slope 1 lines can cover up to floor((n+1)/2) points if we include points from column 1 as well. But if we exclude column 1 (vertical line covering many points), the maximum may be less.\n\nThus the maximum coverage per sunny line is limited but can be up to about floor((n+1)/2) - 1 maybe. So we need to consider if k>3 could be possible.\n\nThe solution's answer is k ∈ {0,1,3}. Let's test for n=6 if k=4 is possible. We need 6 lines total, exactly 4 sunny lines, 2 non-sunny lines. The non-sunny lines could be vertical lines covering columns with many points. For n=6, we have columns with counts: 6,5,4,3,2,1. We could use vertical lines for columns x=1 and x=2 (non-sunny). That covers 6+5=11 points. Remaining points: columns x=3..6: counts 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. We have 4 sunny lines left to cover 10 points. Average 2.5 points per line. Since some sunny lines can cover up to 3 points, maybe possible. Let's try to find a covering.\n\nRemaining points:\n\nx=3: (3,1),(3,2),(3,3),(3,4)\nx=4: (4,1),(4,2),(4,3)\nx=5: (5,1),(5,2)\nx=6: (6,1)\n\nTotal 10 points.\n\nWe need 4 sunny lines to cover them. Let's try to use slope 1 lines that cover 3 points each. For diff -1: points (3,2),(4,3),(5,4?) but (5,4) not allowed because 5+4=9 >7? Actually n=6, a+b ≤7, so (5,4) not allowed. So diff -1 includes (3,2),(4,3) only (2 points). diff 0 includes (3,3),(4,4?) not allowed, (5,5?) not allowed. So only (3,3) alone. diff -2 includes (3,1),(4,2),(5,3?) not allowed. So (3,1),(4,2) only. diff -3 includes (4,1),(5,2),(6,3?) not allowed. So (4,1),(5,2) only. diff -4 includes (5,1),(6,2?) not allowed. So (5,1) alone. diff -5 includes (6,1) alone.\n\nThus slope 1 lines cover at most 2 points for these columns (except maybe diff -1 includes (3,2),(4,3) only). So slope 1 lines not enough.\n\nWhat about slope -2 lines? Let's compute c = y + 2x for points in columns x=3..6:\n\n(3,1): 1+6=7\n(3,2): 2+6=8\n(3,3): 3+6=9\n(3,4): 4+6=10\n(4,1): 1+8=9\n(4,2): 2+8=10\n(4,3): 3+8=11\n(5,1): 1+10=11\n(5,2): 2+10=12\n(6,1): 1+12=13\n\nSo c values: 7: (3,1) only; 8: (3,2) only; 9: (3,3),(4,1) -> line y = -2x + 9 passes through (3,3) and (4,1). That's 2 points. c=10: (3,4),(4,2) -> line y = -2x + 10 passes through (3,4) and (4,2). That's 2 points. c=11: (4,3),(5,1) -> line y = -2x + 11 passes through (4,3) and (5,1). That's 2 points. c=12: (5,2) only. c=13: (6,1) only. So slope -2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nWhat about slope 2 lines? Compute c = y - 2x:\n\n(3,1): 1-6=-5\n(3,2): 2-6=-4\n(3,3): 3-6=-3\n(3,4): 4-6=-2\n(4,1): 1-8=-7\n(4,2): 2-8=-6\n(4,3): 3-8=-5\n(5,1): 1-10=-9\n(5,2): 2-10=-8\n(6,1): 1-12=-11\n\nc values: -5: (3,1),(4,3) -> line y = 2x -5 passes through (3,1) and (4,3). That's 2 points. c=-4: (3,2) only. c=-3: (3,3) only. c=-2: (3,4) only. c=-7: (4,1) only. c=-6: (4,2) only. c=-9: (5,1) only. c=-8: (5,2) only. c=-11: (6,1) only.\n\nThus slope 2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nWhat about slope 1/2 lines? Compute c = (2y - x)/2:\n\n(3,1): (2-3)/2 = -0.5\n(3,2): (4-3)/2 = 0.5\n(3,3): (6-3)/2 = 1.5\n(3,4): (8-3)/2 = 2.5\n(4,1): (2-4)/2 = -1\n(4,2): (4-4)/2 = 0\n(4,3): (6-4)/2 = 1\n(5,1): (2-5)/2 = -1.5\n(5,2): (4-5)/2 = -0.5\n(6,1): (2-6)/2 = -2\n\nSo c values: -0.5: (3,1),(5,2) -> line y = 0.5x -0.5 passes through (3,1) and (5,2). That's 2 points. 0.5: (3,2) only. 1.5: (3,3) only. 2.5: (3,4) only. -1: (4,1) only. 0: (4,2) only. 1: (4,3) only. -1.5: (5,1) only. -2: (6,1) only.\n\nThus at most 2 points.\n\nWhat about slope -1/2 lines? Compute c = (2y + x)/2:\n\n(3,1): (2+3)/2 = 2.5\n(3,2): (4+3)/2 = 3.5\n(3,3): (6+3)/2 = 4.5\n(3,4): (8+3)/2 = 5.5\n(4,1): (2+4)/2 = 3\n(4,2): (4+4)/2 = 4\n(4,3): (6+4)/2 = 5\n(5,1): (2+5)/2 = 3.5\n(5,2): (4+5)/2 = 4.5\n(6,1): (2+6)/2 = 4\n\nc values: 2.5: (3,1) only. 3: (4,1) only. 3.5: (3,2),(5,1) -> line y = -0.5x + 3.5 passes through (3,2) and (5,1). That's 2 points. 4: (4,2),(6,1) -> line y = -0.5x + 4 passes through (4,2) and (6,1). That's 2 points. 4.5: (3,3),(5,2) -> line y = -0.5x + 4.5 passes through (3,3) and (5,2). That's 2 points. 5: (4,3) only. 5.5: (3,4) only.\n\nThus at most 2 points.\n\nThus for the remaining points after covering columns 1 and 2 with vertical lines, each sunny line can cover at most 2 points (except maybe some slope 1 lines covering 3 points if we include column 3? Actually we saw diff -1 line y = x - 1 covers (3,2),(4,3) only, not 3 points. So max 2 points). So we have 10 points to cover with 4 sunny lines, each covering at most 2 points, so max coverage is 8 points, insufficient. So k=4 impossible for n=6 with vertical lines covering columns 1 and 2. Could we choose different non-sunny lines? Perhaps we could use a horizontal line y=1 as a non-sunny line covering many points across columns. Let's try to use horizontal line y=1 (non-sunny) covering points (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1),(6,1). That's 6 points. Then we have 5 lines left (since total lines =6, we used 1 non-sunny line, we need 4 sunny lines and 1 more non-sunny line? Actually we need exactly k=4 sunny lines, so we need 2 non-sunny lines total. So we have one more non-sunny line left. We have 5 lines left: 4 sunny, 1 non-sunny. We need to cover remaining points: all points except those with y=1. Let's list remaining points:\n\nPoints with y>1:\n\nx=1: (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5) (since 1+5=6 ≤7)\nx=2: (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(2,5)\nx=3: (3,2),(3,3),(3,4)\nx=4: (4,2),(4,3)\nx=5: (5,2)\nx=6: none (since y=1 only)\n\nTotal remaining points: 4+4+3+2+1 = 14 points. We have 5 lines left (4 sunny, 1 non-sunny) to cover 14 points. That's average 2.8 points per line. Since sunny lines can cover up to 3 points (maybe more), maybe possible. Let's try to find a covering.\n\nWe can use a second non-sunny line maybe vertical x=1 covering (1,2),(1,3),(1,4),(1,5). That's 4 points. Then we have 4 sunny lines left to cover the rest: points in columns x=2..5 with y≥2 (except those already covered). Let's list them after covering x=1 vertical and y=1 horizontal:\n\nRemaining points:\n\nx=2: (2,2),(2,3),(2,4),(2,5)\nx=3: (3,2),(3,3),(3,4)\nx=4: (4,2),(4,3)\nx=5: (5,2)\n\nTotal 4+3+2+1 = 10 points. We have 4 sunny lines to cover 10 points, average 2.5 points per line. Could be possible if some lines cover 3 points.\n\nLet's try to find sunny lines covering 3 points each. For slope 1 lines (diff b-a):\n\n(2,2): diff 0\n(2,3): diff 1\n(2,4): diff 2\n(2,5): diff 3\n(3,2): diff -1\n(3,3): diff 0\n(3,4): diff 1\n(4,2): diff -2\n(4,3): diff -1\n(5,2): diff -3\n\nSo diff 0: (2,2),(3,3) -> 2 points. diff 1: (2,3),(3,4) -> 2 points. diff -1: (3,2),(4,3) -> 2 points. diff -2: (4,2) only. diff -3: (5,2) only. diff 2: (2,4) only. diff 3: (2,5) only.\n\nThus slope 1 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nSlope -2 lines (c = y + 2x):\n\n(2,2): 2+4=6\n(2,3): 3+4=7\n(2,4): 4+4=8\n(2,5): 5+4=9\n(3,2): 2+6=8\n(3,3): 3+6=9\n(3,4): 4+6=10\n(4,2): 2+8=10\n(4,3): 3+8=11\n(5,2): 2+10=12\n\nc values: 6: (2,2) only. 7: (2,3) only. 8: (2,4),(3,2) -> line y = -2x + 8 passes through (2,4) and (3,2). That's 2 points. 9: (2,5),(3,3) -> line y = -2x + 9 passes through (2,5) and (3,3). That's 2 points. 10: (3,4),(4,2) -> line y = -2x + 10 passes through (3,4) and (4,2). That's 2 points. 11: (4,3) only. 12: (5,2) only.\n\nThus slope -2 lines cover at most 2 points.\n\nSlope 2 lines (c = y - 2x):\n\n(2,2): 2-4=-2\n(2,3): 3-4=-1\n(2,4): 4-4=0\n(2,5): 5-4=1\n(3,2): 2-6=-4\n(3,3): 3-6=-3\n(3,4): 4-6=-2\n(4,2): 2-8=-6\n(4,3): 3-8=-5\n(5,2): 2-10=-8\n\nc values: -2: (2,2),(3,4) -> line y = 2x -2 passes through (2,2) and (3,4). That's 2 points. -1: (2,3) only. 0: (2,4) only. 1: (2,5) only. -4: (3,2) only. -3: (3,3) only. -6: (4,2) only. -5: (4,3) only. -8: (5,2) only.\n\nThus at most 2 points.\n\nSlope 1/2 lines (c = (2y - x)/2):\n\n(2,2): (4-2)/2 = 1\n(2,3): (6-2)/2 = 2\n(2,4): (8-2)/2 = 3\n(2,5): (10-2)/2 = 4\n(3,2): (4-3)/2 = 0.5\n(3,3): (6-3)/2 = 1.5\n(3,4): (8-3)/2 = 2.5\n(4,2): (4-4)/2 = 0\n(4,3): (6-4)/2 = 1\n(5,2): (4-5)/2 = -0.5\n\nc values: 1: (2,2),(4,3) -> line 